      text: "For continuously differentiable f and g the integral of fg′ is the boundary term minus the integral of f′g; for continuously differentiable φ and continuous f the substitution rule holds.",
      proof: "Integrate the product rule (fg)′ = f′g + fg′ and the chain rule (F ∘ φ)′ = (f ∘ φ)φ′ for an antiderivative F of f, using the fundamental theorem of calculus.",
