      proofName: "Inverse function theorem for (x, y) ↦ (x, F(x, y))",
      formula: String.raw`F(x, g(x)) = 0,\quad Dg = -(D_y F)^{-1} D_x F`,
      text: "If F: ℝ<sup>n</sup> × ℝ<sup>m</sup> → ℝ<sup>m</sup> is continuously differentiable, F(a, b) = 0 and the partial derivative D<sub>y</sub>F(a, b) is invertible, then near (a, b) the zeros of F are the graph of a continuously differentiable function y = g(x).",
      proof: "The map (x, y) ↦ (x, F(x, y)) has invertible derivative at (a, b); its local inverse has the form (x, z) ↦ (x, h(x, z)), and g(x) = h(x, 0). The formula for Dg follows from the chain rule.",
      concepts: ["total-derivative", "partial-derivative"],
